{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"## Session close 2026-09-14 — Wave 3 exactly half judged, clean state for handoff\n\nEvery Wave 3 run with all three arms complete is now scored: **10 of 20**, no unscored backlog,\nworking tree clean.\n\n### Pooled over the 10 scored runs\n\n| arm | TP | FP | precision | materiality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Compound Engineering | 161 | 2 | 98.8% | 76% |\n| superpowers | 137 | 1 | 99.3% | 78% |\n| mini-swe-agent | 59 | 3 | 95.2% | 80% |\n\nHead-to-head **5 CE / 5 SP**. **CE:superpowers true positives 1.18x, against 2.42x in Wave 1.**\nMean Fleiss 0.711 with **zero contested items on every panel** — where agreement is low it is\n2-1 splits on borderline nit-vs-TP calls, never a majority in doubt. The two numbers must be\nquoted together or a run looks shakier than it is.\n\n### The cost side moved, and it moved against CE\n\nThree thunderbird personas reported exact token counts, each with a Wave 1 counterpart on the\nsmall Python repo: coherence 1.98x, design-lens 1.82x, scope-guardian 1.92x — **mean 1.91x**. A\ntight spread across three independent personas makes this a property of the corpus, not of a\npersona: cost scales with the tree searched, not the document handed over. Projected to seven\npersonas plus this run's orchestrator, one CE run on a large repo is roughly **an order of\nmagnitude** above superpowers on the same plan, versus the 7.0x measured on Wave 1.\n\nSo on unfamiliar code CE's recall lead narrows sharply while its cost premium widens. Both\ndirections at once. Recorded with its limits: four personas still unmeasured, figures come from\nresumed re-emissions, one run, largest repo class.\n\n### One run quarantined rather than scored\n\n`kotlin-thunderbird-android17-behaviour` CE produced a **4-of-7** persona fan-out. The\norchestrator disclosed it honestly, but the cause is a delivery fault, not a persona failure:\nall three \"not recovered\" personas ran and returned grounded JSON to the top level instead of to\ntheir orchestrator. Scoring it would bias CE downward for an invisible harness reason, so it is\nmoved to `superseded/short-fanout/` with both recovery options costed. A harness-side merge of\nthe recovered personas is explicitly ruled out — CE's cross-persona anchor promotion is part of\nits method and a merge would be a different instrument wearing this one's name.\n\n### Free result on the side\n\nQ3's superpowers cycle 1 turned out to be a **fourth full-material replicate** on\n`2948-48e5-BLOCK` (byte-identical input). The series is now findings 12/13/14/14, blockers\n3/3/6/4, blocking 8/8/11/10 — volume stable, severity not, on four draws instead of three. It\nalso weakened the content-agreement claim honestly: the new run missed the absent `CachePoint`\nthat all three earlier runs found, so that is three-of-four, not unanimous.\n\n### Handoff\n\n`HANDOFF.md` now opens on Wave 3 with an ordered next-actions list. Action 0 is the thunderbird\ndecision. Corrected in it this session: the CE cost guidance (the familiar ~300K figure was an\norchestrator-only floor being read as a run cost), both CE failure modes with their recovery\nrules, and two uncorrected instrument defects carried as explicit caveats rather than fixed —\nmini never saw the declared-file-impact section on any Wave 1 plan, and `harness/runs.json`\ncarries the answer key in the record the arms resolve from."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789415424911427001,"uuid":"2cf4802b-627f-4bb5-9525-e576500d8e76"}